ecord is held and named to the operator, and nothing is lost while it waits. The positions a record names itself are a fixed order, so the drain resumes inside them from its cursor across as many batches as they need. A listing is no such order — a fragment that drops out of it moves every later one — so a batch that reaches a chunk's listed fragments reads all of them, and progress is counted a whole chunk at a time; a chunk listed with more fragments than one batch reads is held and named to the operator as needing a human. A batch lists the fleet at most once, so a pass lists it at most once per batch it commits, plus once per aborted upload whose damaged record is held after its listing. One gap is stated rather than hidden: a write the aborted upload had authorized that lands after the listing, at a position the damaged record does not name, has neither record nor grace record, and GC keeps it — kept for good, never lost, never reclaimed without an operator. A part record that will not decode names no chunk in its key, so it is held for a human, and the upload stays until one acts. A grace record the walk wrote over a position whose write never landed stays as well, since that write may still land there.
 
