he commit lands and that upload is ended together with the part it has just committed. Closing that needs a change to the upload protocol (proposal 0016) that this release does not make; it is a known gap, and the follow-up that closes it must land before uploads are created in production. Each commit is measured against the batch budgets before it is sent and never split. An operator can run exactly one such pass and exit with `wyrd custodian --reap-abandoned-uploads`: it needs no `--endpoints`, reads and writes metadata only, and drains what it ended record by record, holding for a whole-fleet pass anything that could only be retired by listing the fleet; it exits non-zero when anything was held, a commit's outcome is unknown or the profile check refused, so an open upload under another clock keeps it failing until an operator ends that upload. It is refused before it opens the store when given a flag that selects work it does not run — `--endpoints`, `--ids`, `--failure-domains` or `--gc-expired-pending` — rather than ignoring the flag, and when its `--data-dir` holds no local metadata store — no file there, or an empty one — which it never creates: a store made on the spot would be reported as holding nothing to reap. Its last line names the store it ran on: the local file, or the TiKV PD endpoints or the FoundationDB cluster file it connected with.
